{"id": "143794", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143794", "title": "Soroti", "text": "Soroti is a city in Eastern Uganda that was angulated in 2020. It serves as the primary urban, business, and administrative hub within the delineated area of Soroti District, one of the nine administrative districts in the Teso sub-region.\nUnderstand.\nSoroti City is situated within Soroti District, located in the Teso sub-region of the Eastern Region in Uganda, to the north of Lake Kyoga. Soroti is approximately 103 kilometers (64 miles) away from the city of Mbale, which is the largest urban center in Uganda's Eastern Region. It is positioned along the Tororo\u2013Mbale\u2013Soroti Road, which is also part of the Lira-Mbale route, and is approximately 326 kilometers (203 miles) northeast of Kampala, Uganda's capital and largest city.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nWhen coming to Soroti by air, you will actually arrive in the city of Entebbe, 35 km (22 mi) southwest of Kampala.\nYou can get from Entebbe airport to Soroti:\nGet around.\nSoroti city is quite spread out from Soroti Airstrip to the Soroti Rock, and if you intend to go shopping to the several supermarkets in the city like OM supermarket, Goalman supermarket, Dubai shopping center and many more, you can grab a motorcycle that can move around the city at Shs 2000-5000.\nGo next.\nLira or Mbale"}
{"id": "143798", "revid": "113846", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143798", "title": "Fir of Hotova National Park", "text": "Fir of Hotova National Park (Albanian: \"Parku Kombetar \u201cBredhi i Hotoves-Dangelli\u201d\") is near Permet in Southeastern Albania of Albania.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nWith a coverage area of 34,361 hectares consisting mainly of Bulgarian Firs, the area was declared a national park by the Albanian Government on January 15, 1996 under the jurisdiction of the Permet District Forest Service, and National Agency of Protected Areas of Albana (AKZM).\nThe area is by far the most protected forest massif in Albania, successfully resisting any damage or deforestation by man.\nLandscape.\nThe impressive view of the park can be seen from Lumica Valley entrance up to Kokojka Mt summit. At the height of 800-1000 m, there are vast meadows surrounded by centuries-old large trees forming an impressive greenbelt. There are no food facilities in the park, though food can be brought or ordered at nearby villages. Water can be secured from fresh water fountains located on the roadside along the way. \nFlora and fauna.\nFir of Hotova contains the largest area of Bulgarian Fir in the Balkans. Other species include maple, black horn-beam, ilex, red and black juniper, and raspberry.\nSome of the park\u2019s fauna includes the brown bear, wolf, fox, wild boar, hare, squirrel, and wild goat. Deer can also be seen on the pastures deep inside the forest.\nGet in.\nThe road leading to the park from SH75 in Piskova is in rough shape and requires a 4x4.\nFees and permits.\nThere are no entry fees. For assistance, please contact:"}
{"id": "143800", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143800", "title": "Masindi", "text": "Masindi is in Western Uganda.\nUnderstand.\nMasindi is a town in Western Uganda and the capital of Masindi District. It is an important agricultural and commercial centre and serves as a gateway to Murchison Falls National Park, one of Uganda's most famous wildlife destinations.\nMasindi is located between northern and western Uganda and has historically been an important trading centre. The town serves surrounding farming communities that produce crops such as sugarcane, maize, beans, cassava, coffee and other agricultural products.\nThe economy of Masindi is based mainly on agriculture, trade, transport and tourism. Its location near Murchison Falls National Park makes it a popular stop for travellers heading to the park.\nThe area is part of the historic Bunyoro region and has strong cultural links with the Bunyoro-Kitara Kingdom.\nHistory.\nMasindi developed as an administrative and commercial centre during the colonial period. Its location along routes connecting different parts of Uganda helped it grow as a regional trading town.\nThe town has long been connected with agriculture and the movement of goods between western and northern Uganda.\nFollowing Uganda's independence in 1962, Masindi continued to develop as a regional centre and later became an important tourism gateway because of its proximity to Murchison Falls National Park.\nGet in.\nMasindi is 215 km from Kampala and 57.3 km from Hoima."}
{"id": "143831", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143831", "title": "Tabora", "text": "Tabora is in Northwest Tanzania.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nAs the long-distance trains are notoriously delayed and the schedule may change on short notice, it is best to get up-to-date information from the railway station of departure directly.\nThe distance from Tabora to Mwanza is 378 kms by rail. from Tabora to Kigoma is 506 kms by rail. and from Tabora to Dodoma is 347 kms by rail. from Tabora to Mpanda is 380 kms by rail.\nGet around.\nA taxi in the centre costs no more than TSh 2000. A boda-boda (bicycle taxi) starts from 1000 inside the city (Feb 2019).\nSleep.\nTabora has an abundance of guest houses, lodges and hotels in all price ranges."}
{"id": "143832", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143832", "title": "Mpulungu", "text": "Mpulungu is in Zambia, at the southern tip of Lake Tanganyika.\nGet in.\nThe town is the southern terminus for the ferry MV Liemba; the northern terminus is Kigoma in Tanzania.\nbuses and vans to and from Kasama. then train to Kapiri Mposhi or Tanzania.\nTravel to Mpulungu from Kasama is 203 km and takes 3 hours."}
{"id": "143844", "revid": "2247317", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143844", "title": "Ride sharing", "text": "\"Not to be confused with Ride hailing services, commercial ride sharing services like Uber.\"\nRide sharing is an alternative to hitchhiking. Like hitchhiking, it involves seeking a ride in a vehicle operated by another traveller who is going to the same destination. Unlike hitchhiking, the arrangements are usually made in advance; the passenger makes some token payment to the driver to defray or partially defray the cost of the trip.\nUnderstand.\nRidesharing means more ecological and economical transport compared to single occupancy car travel as more than one person ride in a vehicle meaning less consumption of fuel per person transport kilometer and enabling higher traffic density. These services have mobile optimized webapps and also many have native mobile apps.\nIn some countries the legal status of ride sharing with any compensation to the driver is unclear, as it may be regarded as an illegal taxi. The probability of problems for a passenger is low, but if you are going to offer ride sharing for money, you should check this. As licensing taxis is in the jurisdiction of local municipalities in many places, the situation can vary drastically from city to city.\nLocate.\nVarious Peer-to-peer ridesharing media are useful to locate prospective drivers or passengers for ride sharing:\nRide sharing is most likely to be successful between major centres or popular, beaten-path destinations. On long trips, such as a cross-country Trans-Canada Highway run, passengers may be asked to do some of the long haul driving. It's best to post a request for transportation well in advance and start checking for offer listings at least a week prior to the desired departure dates. It also helps to be flexible with departure and arrival times. As with hitchhiking, some common sense and discretion is advisable.\nAlternatives.\nRide sharing is not to be confused with:"}
{"id": "143845", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143845", "title": "Mpanda", "text": "Mpanda is a \"frontier town\" of more than 204,000 people (2022) in Northwest Tanzania.\nUnderstand.\nMpanda is an important centre in the\u00a0rural economy, especially for the marketing and transshipment of rice and maize. The Katavi region is increasingly of interest to mineral prospectors, especially for gold. \nGet in.\nMpanda is roughly 500\u00a0km north of\u00a0Mbeya\u00a0and 380\u00a0km south-west of\u00a0Tabora.\nBy plane.\nMpanda has a small domestic airport with flights from a few other cities in Tanzania.\nBy train.\nThere are two trains a week Thursday/Sunday 13:00 from Mpanda to Tabora, operated by TRC. Journey times are 14 hours. the distance from Mpanda to Tabora by rail is 380 km.\nBy bus.\nThere are buses between Mpanda and Sumbawanga, which is 6-7 hours to the south. From Mpanda to Sumbawanga is 239 km.\nThere are also buses between Mpanda and Tabora but the sleeper train is a better way to travel.\nThere are also buses going between Mpanda and Kigoma.\nBuy.\nNBC bank has a Visa card ATM.\nCRDB (15,000 Tsh fee) and NMB banks have Mastercard/Visa card ATMs.\nGo next.\nSumbawanga, Tobora, Tunduma."}
{"id": "143865", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143865", "title": "Parksville", "text": "Parksville is a city in Central Vancouver Island in British Columbia. Informally considered a twin city with neighbouring Qualicum Beach, it's a popular vacation area in summer with a number of provincial parks, sandy beaches, and family fun parks.\nUnderstand.\nService sector and resource processing jobs make up the largest part of the Parksville economy. The three largest types of occupations in Parksville are the service industry; the construction industry; and the business, financial, and administration industries. These are the largest sectors of the city's economy largely due to the summer tourism industry, and a large influx of retirees moving into the area. There is also a fair amount of fishing and forestry in the surrounding areas of Parksville. There is an industrial park in the south-east section of the city, which includes many small tradesman shops.\nHistory.\nThe Spanish were the first Europeans to explore the area in 1791. In Spanish maps, the Englishman River is called \"Rio de Grullas,\" (River of Cranes, i.e. herons) while French Creek is named \"Punta de Leonardo.\"\nThe first European settler in the Parksville area was John Hirst, who pre-empted 120 hectares (300 acres) of land on both sides of the Englishman River in 1873. The River, as it was known, remained an outpost until the turn of the century when a road was built from Nanaimo.\nA post office followed in 1877, with mail distributed from the cabin of Nelson Parks (to which Parksville owes its namesake). At that time, logging was a major industry and the extension of the Esquimalt and Nanaimo (E &amp; N) Railway in 1901, to McBride Junction spurred Parksville\u2019s growth and the beginning of a tourism boom, mainly Vancouver Islanders who travelled to Parksville to enjoy the beaches. Motels, stores, resorts and campgrounds soon followed.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nParksville is located just off of the Island Highway (Highway 19), about 30 minutes north of Nanaimo, take Exits 46 and 51. The nearest ferry terminal with connections to Vancouver and the Lower Mainland is in Nanaimo."}
{"id": "143867", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=143867", "title": "Nyeri", "text": "Nyeri is in Southern Rift Valley.\nGet in.\nMany buses and matatus go from \nBy rail.\nThe railway line between Nairobi and Nanyuki has been rehabilitated and 2x weekly passenger trains have return. However, travelling by train is still slower compared to taking the bus. The station is located in the village of Kiganjo, about six kilometres northeast of the city."}
